Fixes #64733.

When a class with a downleveled `static {}` block refers to its own name, the block's `this` is rewritten to the class alias (`_a`). Subtrees that contain no class fields and no lexical `this` are visited with `substitutionVisitor`, which only replaces references to the class name. But `visitPropertyAccessExpressionForSubstitution` visited the expression with `tx.Visitor()` instead of the active visitor, so `this.bar()` inside a regular function in the block reached `visitThisExpression` while the static block was still the current class element, and became `_a.bar()`:

```ts
class Foo {
    declare toObject: () => number;
    bar() { return 1; }
    static {
        Foo.prototype.toObject = function (this: Foo) {
            return this.bar(); // emitted as `_a.bar()` for target < es2022
        };
    }
}
```

This passes the active visitor into `visitPropertyAccessExpressionForSubstitution`: `substitutionVisitor` from `visitForSubstitution`, and `tx.Visitor()` from `visitPropertyAccessExpression`. The behavior came in with the original port of `visitPropertyAccessExpressionForSubstitution` (microsoft/typescript-go#3147).

The new test `classStaticBlock29` covers a function and an arrow inside a function nested in the block, alongside `this` directly in the block (still rewritten to `_a`) and an element access (not affected). Its ES2021 output matches TypeScript 6.0.3.

